  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 328 AM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nLow pressure, currently located in northern Ontario, will be \npropagating east today as a 500mb shortwave trough moves downstream. \nAn occluded front associated with this sfc low has been sweeping \nthrough the CWA, with the main lingering impact early this morning \nbeing low cloud cover and patchy drizzle. Northwest post-frontal \nflow is expected to occur today, ushering in a drier airmass from \nCanada and decreasing cloud cover. There will be the potential for \nscattered light rain showers in the Arrowhead this morning, with \nmodel soundings from CAMs indicating weak low-level instability and \nsaturation. By the afternoon, precipitation is expected to come to \nan end as dry air advection continues. \n\nNorthwest winds will be easing this evening as high pressure briefly \nbuilds over the Upper Mississippi River Valley. These light winds \ncombined with diurnal cooling will likely lead to patchy fog \ndevelopment tonight into early Monday morning. Some of this fog may \nbecome dense for the Monday morning commute, with the 00z HREF \nshowing a 25-35% chance for visibility of 1/2 mile of less from St. \nLouis County to the I-35 corridor and across northwest Wisconsin. \n\nThe next chance for showers and thunderstorms arrives on Monday into \nMonday night as a 500mb shortwave and attendant sfc cold front move \ninto the CWA. Expect cloud cover to increase on Monday morning, with \nshowers and thunderstorms likely to arrive to the western CWA on \nMonday afternoon. These showers and thunderstorms will move from \nwest to east across the area through the afternoon and evening as \nthe cold front sweeps through. A few of these storms may become \nstrong, especially in the Brainerd Lakes area, capable of producing \nsmall hail and gusty winds. The better chance for severe \nthunderstorms will mostly likely be southwest of the CWA in \nsouthwest MN, with our neck of the woods being on the far northern \nperiphery of the highest CAPE values and deep moisture.\n\nFollowing the cold frontal passage on Monday night, a drier pattern \nis expected to settle over the CWA for Tuesday through Wednesday \nnight. Of note will be unseasonably cool temperatures, especially on \nTuesday night into early Wednesday morning where low temps in the \nlow 40s are forecast. The latter half of the workweek will see \nadditional chances for showers and thunderstorms as the pattern \naloft remains active. High pressure remains favored for next \nweekend, with high temps ranging from the mid 70s to low 80s.",
